+Three traps this encodes:
+  * drafter weights: a drafter pinned to a GPU emits the SAME line shapes as the
+    target model, AFTER it. `model`/`state` therefore SUM per device (agg=True);
+    last-wins reported the drafter as the model (3313 MiB target shown as 656).
   * moe-cache pool: take the LAST report per (device, pool index). A drafter load
     triggers a SECOND allocation and both are logged; summing all matches
     double-counts (measured 13,947 against a live 6,820 -- 2.05x).
   * A pool figure read at boot is a LOWER BOUND. The pool grows into free VRAM
     as traffic admits experts, until it hits the free-minus-reserve floor.
 
+Known residual: `compute` is still last-wins. The target re-reserves after the
+drafter loads, and the log does not label which context a `sched_reserve` line
+belongs to, so a strictly-smaller interleaved reservation (the drafter's
+compute) cannot be distinguished from a re-reserve of the same buffer without
+an engine-side label. Its drafter share lands in `unaccounted`.
+
 Scope: the BYTE split only. Pool slot counts, hit rates and cache health belong to
 CAPTURE: EXPERT CACHE and are deliberately not duplicated here -- one owner per
 number, so the two sections can never disagree.
